    <div class="main-article-body article-content">
      <!-- Key Takeaways Callout -->
      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-title">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="currentColor"><path d="M9 16.17L4.83 12l-1.42 1.41L9 19 21 7l-1.41-1.41z"/></svg>
          Executive Summary: Workers' Comp Fundamentals
        </div>
        <ul class="callout-list">
          <li><strong>The Statutory Mandate:</strong> In almost every US state (with Texas being the primary exception for private non-subscriber employers), carrying workers' compensation insurance becomes mandatory the moment you hire your first non-owner employee.</li>
          <li><strong>The Rating Formula:</strong> Workers' comp premiums are calculated strictly using: <strong>(Total Gross Payroll &divide; $100) &times; NCCI Class Code Rate &times; Experience Modification Rate (E-Mod)</strong>.</li>
          <li><strong>Severe Penalties for Misclassification:</strong> Deliberately misclassifying high-risk construction roofers as low-risk clerical office staff constitutes statutory insurance fraud, triggering massive state stop-work orders and treble damage fines.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p class="lead-text">
        Workers' compensation insurance is a state-regulated "grand bargain" established over a century ago: In exchange for guaranteed, no-fault medical coverage and partial wage replacement after workplace injuries, employees surrender the common-law right to sue their employer for workplace negligence.
      </p>

      <h2>Representative NCCI Class Code Rates per $100 of Payroll</h2>
      <p>
        The National Council on Compensation Insurance (NCCI) establishes 4-digit classification codes grouping occupations by workplace physical hazard. Below are typical rate benchmarks across common small business occupations:
      </p>

      <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Class Code</th>
              <th>Occupational Description</th>
              <th>Risk Level</th>
              <th>Avg. Rate per $100 Payroll</th>
              <th>Annual Premium for $100k Payroll</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>8810</strong></td>
              <td>Clerical Office Employees / Remote Tech Workers</td>
              <td>Very Low</td>
              <td>$0.18 – $0.28</td>
              <td>$180 – $280 / yr</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>8742</strong></td>
              <td>Outside Sales / Real Estate Agents</td>
              <td>Low</td>
              <td>$0.35 – $0.55</td>
              <td>$350 – $550 / yr</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>8017</strong></td>
              <td>Retail Store Sales & Cashiers</td>
              <td>Moderate</td>
              <td>$1.20 – $1.85</td>
              <td>$1,200 – $1,850 / yr</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>9079</strong></td>
              <td>Restaurant Food Servers & Kitchen Staff</td>
              <td>Moderate-High</td>
