            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>5403</strong></td>
              <td>Carpentry / Residential Construction Framing</td>
              <td>High</td>
              <td>$6.80 – $10.50</td>
              <td>$6,800 – $10,500 / yr</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>5551</strong></td>
              <td>Roofing & Solar Panel Installation</td>
              <td>Very High</td>
              <td>$14.00 – $22.00</td>
              <td>$14,000 – $22,000 / yr</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>How Your Experience Modification Rating (E-Mod) Multiplies or Cuts Rates</h2>
      <p>
        Once your business reaches a certain premium threshold (typically $5,000 to $10,000 annually), state rating bureaus assign you an <strong>Experience Modification Rating (E-Mod)</strong>.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Baseline E-Mod (1.00):</strong> Your safety record perfectly mirrors your industry average. You pay standard rates.</li>
        <li><strong>Favorable E-Mod (e.g. 0.80):</strong> If your business implements strict safety protocols and experiences zero lost-time claims over 3 years, your E-Mod drops to 0.80, granting you an automatic <strong>20% discount</strong> across your entire workers' comp bill.</li>
        <li><strong>Adverse E-Mod (e.g. 1.45):</strong> If you experience frequent injuries, your E-Mod spikes, forcing you to pay an extra <strong>45% surcharge</strong>. Furthermore, many commercial developers reject subcontractor bids from contractors with an E-Mod above 1.00.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What Is a "Ghost Policy" for Sole Proprietors?</h2>
      <p>
        If you are a solo independent contractor or single-member LLC in the construction or landscaping trades, you have no employees to insure. However, general contractors and commercial clients often refuse to hire you unless you furnish a Workers' Comp Certificate of Insurance.
      </p>
      <div class="card" style="margin: 1.5rem 0;">
        <h4 style="color: var(--color-primary); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">How a Workers' Comp Ghost Policy Works:</h4>
        <p>
          A <strong>Ghost Policy</strong> (or minimum-premium policy) is a legally valid workers' comp policy where the business owner explicitly excludes themselves from coverage. Because there are no employees and payroll is $0, the policy costs only the state minimum filing premium (typically <strong>$750 to $1,200 annually</strong>).
        </p>
        <p style="margin: 0;">
          It provides a genuine Certificate of Insurance, allowing the solo contractor to bid on commercial contracts while protecting the hiring general contractor from payroll audit surcharges.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>State-by-State Workers' Comp Employee Thresholds</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>1 Employee Required:</strong> California, New York, Illinois, Pennsylvania, Ohio, New Jersey (coverage required immediately upon hiring 1 worker).</li>
        <li><strong>3 Employees Required:</strong> North Carolina, Georgia, Florida (non-construction). In Florida construction trades, coverage is required at 1 employee.</li>
        <li><strong>4 to 5 Employees Required:</strong> Mississippi, Alabama, Missouri, Tennessee.</li>
      </ul>

